==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-sell out.ogg|a=AU}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|<,,sold>}}

# {{senseid|en|sell all available stock, stock out}} To [[sell]] all of a [[supply#Noun|supply]] or [[stock#Noun|stock]] of something.
#: {{syn|en|run out|stock out}}
#: {{ux|en|They '''sold out''' of concert posters yesterday and won't get another shipment until next week.}}
# {{senseid|en|betray principles, intransitively}} {{lb|en|intransitive}} To abandon or [[betray]] one's [[principle]]s to seek [[profit]] or other [[personal]] [[advantage]]; to abandon or betray one's [[supporter]]s through this change.
#: {{ux|en|It used to be a pleasant little community, until the leaders '''sold out''' to the developers.}}
#: {{ux|en|He used to be a voice for the underdog until he '''sold out'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Atlantic|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/entertainment/archive/2019/03/reality-bites-captured-gen-x-25-years-later-helen-childress/583870/|title=''Reality Bites'' Captured Gen X With Perfect Irony|date=2019-03-06|author=Soraya Roberts|passage={{...|As if that weren’t work enough,}} Lelaina is also attempting to choose between two men who represent her divergent prospects: to '''sell out''' or not to '''sell out'''. That particular quandary was styled as a hallmark of Generation X, and ''Reality Bites'' was perhaps the most polished of a bunch of mainstream attempts to portray the ambivalent cohort.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260520014557/https://www.theatlantic.com/entertainment/archive/2019/03/reality-bites-captured-gen-x-25-years-later-helen-childress/583870/|archivedate=20 May 2026}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Chuck Klosterman|title={{w|The Nineties (book)|The Nineties}}|year=2022|publisher=Penguin Press|location=New York|isbn=978-0-7352-1795-9|passage=The nineties were not an age for the aspirant. The worst thing you could be was a sellout, and not because '''selling out''' involved money. '''Selling out''' meant you needed to be popular, and any explicit desire for approval was enough to prove you were terrible.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Caspar Salmon|title=Has Barbie killed the indie director? Why credible film-makers are selling out|date=2023-07-19|url=https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/jul/19/barbie-indie-director-film-maker-greta-gerwig|passage=But the recent writer and actor strikes in the US show that '''selling out''' is not just a question of personal ethics, but an industry-wide concern. A director’s decision to align themselves with these Goliaths of entertainment has consequences; makes money for the big guy, in opposition to fostering an industry where smaller films and creators have more opportunities.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261016123846/https://www.theguardian.com/film/2023/jul/19/barbie-indie-director-film-maker-greta-gerwig|archivedate=16 October 2026}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|author=w:Thomas L. Friedman|title=Trump’s Democracy-for-Diesel Deal Shames Us All|date=2026-10-15|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/15/opinion/trump-russia-oil-putin.html|passage=There is simply no core American value Trump will not '''sell out'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|betray, transitively}} {{lb|en|transitive}} To betray (a person), usually a close friend or family member, for personal gain.
#: {{syn|en|sell|sell the pass|sell down the river|Thesaurus:betray}}
#: {{ux|en|I'll kill him if I see him again; he '''sold''' me '''out'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|sell everything off}} To sell one's [[business]] ''(with a connotative emphasis on entirety and finality)''.
#: {{ux|en|The owners long resisted buyout offers, but when they were ready to retire, they finally '''sold out''' to a competitor.}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}} To [[commit]] or [[abandon]] oneself entirely (to a cause, a team, etc.).
# {{senseid|en|sell one_s military commission}} {{lb|en|historical|military}} To sell one's [[commission]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1857|author=Washington Irving|title=Life of George Washington|volume=2|page=267
|passage=After the peace of Versailles he resided in England; but, about three years before the breaking out of the Revolution, he '''sold out''' his commission in the army and emigrated to New York.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|finance|dated}} To [[liquidate]], [[sell off]], or [[divest]] oneself of financial investments, stocks, or government funds.
#* {{RQ:Austen Sense and Sensibility|volume=II|chapter=XI|passage='{{...}} I might have been very unfortunate indeed; for the stocks were at that time so low, that if I had not happened to have the necessary sum in my banker's hands, I must have '''sold out''' to very great loss. {{...}}'}}
# {{senseid|en|hurry out}} {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic}} To hurry out.

